[Result "1-0"]

 { Radoslaw Wojtaszek – Alexei Fedorov, Czech Republic 2012 Learning objective: This game is an example of White’s f2-f4 reaction against Black’s recapture with ...gxf5. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. Nf3 d6 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 Nc6 7. O-O Bf5 8. d5 Na5 9. Nd2 c5 10. e4 Bd7 11. Qc2 e5 12. b3 Ng4 { Both players follow theory quite closely, and now we are heading to the position of interest. } 13. Bb2 f5 14. exf5 gxf5 { This position has occurred in a total of six games, and White scored four and a half points, so we may guess White’s position is somewhat preferable. Let’s examine the position. Black has captured on f5 with his pawn in order to prevent White from installing a powerful knight on e4. This gives rise to a generally complex position. Black may attempt to: 1. Weaken White’s kingside with ...h7-h5-h4. 2. Transfer his queen and rook to the h-file with attacking chances. 3. Play ...e5-e4 in order to open the h8-a1 diagonal and gain potential outposts on d3 and f3. As previously mentioned, White will often choose between playing f2-f3 or f2-f4. In this case the choice is relatively simple since f2-f3 would block the bishop on g2. As the reader may note, White’s pieces are not well arranged to pursue other plans, such as b2-b4, hence the game continued with: } 15. h3 Nf6 16. f4 e4 { This reaction is pretty much forced in most positions, and now we reach a critical moment in the game. White has only one good plan, which is: prepare the break g3-g4. Meanwhile Black must prevent this plan or he will face a formidable kingside attack. The following moves are rather thematic, but the order in which they are executed can make a dramatic difference, hence they deserve careful calculation. } ( { If } 16... exf4 17. gxf4 $14 { White will make better use of the g-file, due to his spatial edge and Black’s useless knight on a5 . } ) 
17. Nd1 ( { PH: The computer has a radical idea here: it wants to play } 17. Rae1 { followed by a sacrifice on e4, for example } Nh5 18. Ncxe4 Bxb2 19. Qxb2 fxe4 20. Nxe4 Bf5 21. Bf3 Ng7 22. Nf2 { followed by an advance of the kingside pawns. White is clearly better. } ) 
Qe8 { Typical – heading towards g6 to apply pressure down the g-file, and preventing g3-g4. } ( { PH: It's tempting to put the queen on g6, but it turns out to be a mistake, Instead, if } 17... Qe7 18. Ne3 Nh5 19. Bxg7 Qxg7 { Black is fine. } ) 
18. Kh2 $6 { This is not the best move order. } ( { A better move order was: } 18. Ne3 { Intending to meet: } Qg6 { With: } 19. g4 $1 fxg4 20. f5 ( { Or } 20. hxg4 $5 Rae8 $2 ( { better is } 20. Kh2 Bxg4 21. Rae1 Bh5 22. Nxe4 $14 ) 
21. f5 Qg5 22. Rae1 $16 { when White is nearly winning, as in the game Baryshpolets – Turov, Voronezh 2012. } ) 
 { After the text move the complications favour White, for example: } Qg5 21. Nxg4 Bxf5 22. Nxf6 Bxf6 23. Bxf6 Rxf6 24. Nxe4 Bxe4 25. Qxe4 $16 { Black’s main problem is his useless knight on a5 . } ) 
 ( { Instead, after } 18. Ne3 { Black should reply with } h5 { and now White can rearrange his pieces in a desirable way, for example } 19. Kh2 ( 19. g4 $5 { is another option } ) 
Qg6 20. Rg1 Rf7 21. Bf1 $14 { followed by Ng2-h4. } ) 
Qg6 19. Rf2 $6 ( { Clearing f1 to meet } 19. Rf2 Nh5 { with } 20. Nf1 { , but this is not the most accurate. } ) 
 ( { A more effective move order was } 19. Ne3 $5 { since White is better prepared for the complications arising after: } Nh5 20. g4 fxg4 21. f5 $1 { For example: } Bxf5 22. Nxf5 Rxf5 23. Bxe4 Bxb2 24. Qxb2 g3 25. Kg2 Rxf1 26. Rxf1 $14 ) 
Kf7 $2 { Black is playing with the right idea, that is, to place a rook on the g-file to press against the break g3-g4. Unfortunately, this is not the best way to implement this plan. } ( { Much better was } 19... Nh5 20. Nf1 Rf7 { when White’s pieces cannot easily be organized, say after: } 21. Nde3 Bxb2 22. Qxb2 Rg7 23. Bh1 Kf7 ( { but not } 23... Nxg3 $2 { due to } 24. Rg2 Nxf1 25. Rxf1 Qf7 26. Rxg7 Qxg7 27. Rg1 $18 ) 
24. Rg2 Rag8 $15 { It will be difficult to carry out g3-g4, hence White risks being squeezed to death. } ) 
20. Ne3 { From e3 the knight supports g3-g4 and exerts pressure on the f5-pawn. } Rg8 ( { The try } 20... Nh5 { no longer works due to: } 21. g4 $1 Bxb2 ( { or } 21... fxg4 22. hxg4 Bxg4 23. Bxe4 $18 ) 
22. Qxb2 Nf6 23. Nxf5 Bxf5 24. gxf5 Qxf5 25. Re1 $16 ) 
21. Rg1 ( { Unlike the variation } 19. Rg1 Nh5 { , White is now in time to bring his rook to the kingside. } ) 
Bh6 $2 { This is a serious mistake which costs Fedorov the game. He has underestimated the strength of the break g3-g4, and will pay the price. } ( { It was absolutely necessary to play } 21... h5 { preventing g3-g4, but after } 22. Bf1 Ke7 23. Be2 b6 24. Ng2 $5 { White can slowly manoeuvre in order to prepare the g3-g4 break: } Nb7 25. Nh4 Qh7 26. Nf1 $14 { This can be followed by Ne3 and possibly transferring the king to the queenside. This is a very nice position for the reader to analyze; White is certainly calling the shots, but Black’s position is solid for the moment. } ) 
22. Bh1 ( { This is better than } 22. Bf1 { , as the bishop on h1 keeps an eye on the e4-pawn, which will be severely weakened after g3-g4. } ) 
b6 ( { If } 22... Rae8 { then } 23. g4 fxg4 24. Rg3 $1 { +- followed by Bxf6 , which is similar to the game. } ) 
 ( { The try } 22... Bg7 23. g4 Bh8 { does not help either due to the simple } 24. Rff1 $1 { threatening gxf5 or g4-g5 followed by Nxe4 winning. } ) 
23. g4 $1 { +- White has achieved the desired g3-g4 break under perfect circumstances. Black’s position falls apart immediately due to White’s excellent piece coordination, and the vulnerability of Black’s king and the e4-pawn. } fxg4 ( 23... Bf8 { doesn’t help, because after } 24. g5 Ne8 25. Nxe4 $1 { White opens the position decisively, for example: } fxe4 26. Bxe4 Qh5 27. Bf5 $1 Ke7 28. Qe4 Kd8 29. Bxd7 Kxd7 30. Qe6 ) 
24. Rg3 $1 { Preventing ...g4-g3, and preparing the decisive Bxf6 followed by Nxe4; Black is helpless against this threat. } Rae8 25. Bxf6 Qxf6 26. Nxe4 Qe7 27. Nxg4 Bxg4 ( { Even worse is: } 27... Bf8 $2 28. Ng5 Kg7 29. Qxh7 ) 
28. hxg4 Qh4+ 29. Rh3 Qxg4 30. Ng5+ Bxg5 31. Qxh7+ ( { Black resigns. Mate is unavoidable, for example: } 31. Qxh7+ Rg7 32. fxg5 Ke7 33. Qxg7 Kd8 34. Rh7 ) 
 ( { Or } 31. Qxh7+ Kf6 32. fxg5 Ke5 33. Re3 Kd4 34. Qd3 { Final remarks 1. Black’s strategy in this game was generally correct: he exerted pressure along the g-file and attempted to prevent the g3-g4 break. His big failure was missing the precise 19...Nh5 to prevent White’s ideal piece organization. 2. After 19...Kf7, White obtained an ideal piece coordination, and the threat of g3-g4 gained significant strength, securing a comfortable advantage. If the reader wishes to learn more, he should analyze the resulting position after 21...h5, as suggested above. } ) 

 1-0

