Rxg3+ $1 { This is the key, gaining a tempo to bring in the other rook; White just does not have time to organize a defence. } 34. Bxg3 Rg8 35. Qe1 f4 36. Rh1 ( { Or } 36. Ne4 Bh3 37. Kg1 fxg3 { threatening ...Be3 or ...Bxf1 . } ) 
Rxg3+ 37. Qxg3 Qxg3+ 38. Kf1 Bh3+ 39. Rxh3 Qxh3+ { Black has a huge material advantage and the rest is simple. } 40. Kg1 Ng5 41. Nf2 Nxf3+ 42. Bxf3 Qxf3 43. Nce4 Bg5 $1 44. Nxd6 ( { Or: } 44. Nxg5 Qg3 45. Kf1 Qxg5 $19 ) 
Qg3+ 45. Kf1 f3 46. Nf7+ Kg7 { White resigns in view of ...Qg2-g1 winning more material. Final remarks 1. White’s biggest mistake was wasting time with moves 17, 21, 22 and 23, to then get his own queen stuck out of the game. The problem was White’s lack of targets on the queenside. 2. After 26. exf5 gxf5 Black is essentially winning, but not because of the structure. Black is winning because White cannot prevent the knight transfer ...Nh5-f4, and because the queen is still completely sidelined on a4. } 
 0-1

