[Result "1-0"]

 { Sandro Mareco – Axel Bachmann, Santos 2010 Learning objective: This game teaches us how White gains control of the game once light-squared bishops are traded. Note how passive Black’s dark-squared bishop is. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. Nf3 d6 5. e4 O-O 6. Be2 Na6 7. O-O e5 8. Re1 c6 9. Bf1 Bg4 10. d5 c5 11. Bg5 h6 12. Bh4 Bd7 13. Nd2 g5 14. Bg3 h5 15. f3 Bh6 16. Bd3 Ne8 17. Nf1 f5 18. exf5 Bxf5 ( { Attempting to preserve the light-squared bishop with } 18... Ng7 19. Bf2 Nxf5 { is answered by } 20. Bxf5 $1 Bxf5 21. Ng3 Bg6 22. Nge4 $16 { followed by Be3 and possibly g2-g4, blocking the kingside in a similar fashion to the game. } ) 
19. Bxf5 Rxf5 { The light-squared bishops come off the board, allowing White to easily control the key e4-square. In addition after: } 20. Bf2 Ng7 21. Be3 $1 { The g5-pawn is fixed and White will be able to lock Black’s dark-squared bishop out of the game. } Rf6 22. Ne4 Rg6 23. g4 $1 { ± The kingside is blocked and White has not created any weaknesses which Black could exploit. White controls the e4-square and his bishop on e3 is far stronger than Black’s trapped bishop on h6. Hence, White is positionally winning already. } Qd7 24. h3 Rf8 25. Kg2 Nc7 26. Nfg3 h4 27. Nf1 Nce8 ( { Black does not gain much from } 27... b5 28. b3 { as after } Qf7 29. Nfd2 Nge8 30. Qe2 Qd7 31. Rab1 $16 { White will favourably open the queenside with b3-b4 anyway. } ) 
28. Qd3 Kf7 29. Nfd2 Nf6 30. a3 Nge8 31. b4 b6 32. bxc5 bxc5 ( { Even worse is } 32... dxc5 $2 33. Bxc5 $1 bxc5 34. Nxc5 Qd6 35. Ne6 Rfg8 36. Rab1 { followed by Rb7 with a decisive attack. } ) 
 ( { PH: } 32... bxc5 32. Bxc5 { is of course nice, but } 33. a4 { should also be strategically winning for White. } ) 
33. Rab1 Rg7 34. Rb8 Kg8 35. Reb1 ( { And Black resigned in this desperate position. A possible continuation could have been: } 35. Reb1 Nxe4 36. Nxe4 Qf7 37. Rxe8 $1 Qxe8 ( { The queen is trapped after } 37. Reb1 Qxf3 38. Kh2 Rxe8 39. Rf1 ) 
38. Nxd6 Qg6 39. Nf5 Kh7 40. Bxc5 Rc8 41. Be3 { And the central pawns decide the game. Final remarks 1. This game teaches us how grave it is for Black to lose control of the light squares in a King’s Indian Type II-B structure. 2. White’s idea 23. g4! works excellently because it locks Black’s bishop out of the game, and because Black’s pieces are not well arranged to take advantage of the weak f4-square. } ) 

 1-0

