 { Sandro Mareco – Jorge Cori, Buenos Aires 2009 Learning objectives: 1. This game illustrates White’s queenside play with the b2-b4 break. 2. Special attention should be paid to Black’s mistake with 10...a6. } 1. d4 g6 2. c4 Bg7 3. Nf3 d6 4. Nc3 c6 5. e4 e5 6. Be2 Nd7 7. O-O Ne7 { Black’s opening choice aims to obtain a King’s Indian type of position, without the need to know specific opening lines. This strategy is typically double-edged, as it avoids the opponent’s opening preparation, but the resulting position could turn out to be inferior from the start. } 8. d5 $5 { This move forces Black to define his central pawn structure. } ( { Another strong option was } 8. Be3 $5 O-O 9. Qd2 { since the only reaction } f5 { can be met by: } 10. Bg5 ( { or } 10. dxe5 Nxe5 11. Nxe5 Bxe5 12. Bd4 $5 { ⩲ } ) 
Nf6 11. exf5 exd4 12. Nxd4 Nxf5 13. Nxf5 Bxf5 14. Rad1 $14 { With a stable advantage due to the backward d6-pawn. } ) 
c5 { Now Black has obtained a somewhat inferior version of the structure studied in this chapter. Black’s problem is his knight on d7, which blocks the light-squared bishop, hence the plan ...f7-f5 is momentarily ‘banned’ due to the annoying reply Ng5-e6. } ( { Worse is } 8... cxd5 9. cxd5 O-O 10. Be3 $16 { when White obtains a favourable version of the structure studied in the previous chapter, since Black is not ready to fight for the c-file and his kingside counterplay has not started. } ) 
9. a3 { Probably the only move that deserves consideration. White will create queenside pressure with b2-b4. } O-O 10. b4 a6 $6 { One of my hopes in showing this game is that the reader will never make this mistake. This move weakens the b6-square for the rest of the game, and should be avoided if possible. } ( { Correct was } 10... b6 $1 { which is similar to Piorun – Hillarp Persson from this chapter: } 11. Rb1 h6 { followed by ...f7-f5 . Black’s position is perfectly playable } ( { the direct } 11... f5 { struggles against } 12. Ng5 Nf6 13. Bf3 { , preparing Ne6 , } fxe4 14. Ncxe4 $14 ) 
 ) 
11. Rb1 Qc7 { Supporting the c5-pawn. } ( { If Black simply launches an attack with } 11... h6 12. Be3 f5 { then after } 13. bxc5 dxc5 ( { not } 13... f4 $2 14. cxd6 fxe3 15. fxe3 $1 { when White wins two pawns for little compensation } ) 
14. a4 f4 15. Bc1 g5 16. Ne1 { White is positionally winning due to the weakness of the c5-pawn. A sample variation is } Ng6 17. Nd3 Qc7 18. Ba3 b6 19. Qc2 Rd8 20. a5 $1 bxa5 21. Na4 Bf8 22. Bg4 { winning the c5-pawn and the game. } ) 
12. Be3 Rb8 ( 12... f5 $2 { loses to } 13. Ng5 { threatening Ne6 and after } Nf6 14. bxc5 ) 
13. Qb3 { Creating the positional threat of 14.bxc5. } ( { PH: The computer actually wants to attack on the kingside: } 13. Kh1 b6 14. Rg1 $5 { followed by g4, making it as difficult as possible for Black to play ...f5. } ) 
b6 ( { Once again, the try } 13... f5 $2 { is met by: } 14. Ng5 Nf6 15. bxc5 dxc5 16. Na4 $1 b6 17. Nxc5 $18 ) 
 ( { The waiting move } 13... h6 $2 { is met by the strong: } 14. bxc5 Nxc5 ( 14... dxc5 { is also losing after the illustrative variation: } 15. Rfd1 Qd6 16. a4 f5 17. a5 f4 18. Bd2 g5 19. Ne1 Nf6 20. Qa3 Bd7 21. Rb6 ) 
15. Qb6 $1 { Winning a pawn, for example: } Qxb6 16. Rxb6 Rd8 17. Bxc5 dxc5 18. Na4 $18 ) 
14. Rfd1 h6 { Preparing ...f7-f5. } ( { Again } 14... f5 $2 { fails to } 15. Ng5 { followed by Ne6 . } ) 
 { Now that Black has wasted a tempo with 14...h6, White proceeds with the typical manoeuvre: } 15. Ne1 { Heading toward d3 to pressure c5. Moreover, White must be ready to meet ...f7-f5 with f2-f3 in order to maintain the solidity of his position. } f5 ( { Black typically will not consider the option } 15... cxb4 $6 16. axb4 { as it reinforces White’s centre. A possible continuation is: } f5 17. f3 Nf6 18. Nd3 { (preparing c4-c5) } fxe4 19. fxe4 Bg4 20. Nf2 Bxe2 21. Nxe2 $16 { when the positional threat of c4-c5 is hard to prevent. } ) 
16. f3 Bb7 17. Na4 Ba8 18. Nd3 Rb7 ( { Opening the f-file with } 18... fxe4 $6 { does not bring anything good for Black after: } 19. fxe4 Nf6 20. Nc3 Kh7 21. Nf2 g5 22. g3 $16 ) 
 ( { The counter-break } 18... b5 $2 { fails against: } 19. Naxc5 $1 dxc5 20. Nxc5 Qd6 ( { or } 20... Nxc5 21. bxc5 { when the passed pawns decide the game } ) 
21. cxb5 axb5 22. Nxd7 Qxd7 23. d6 $18 ) 
19. Qc3 Rfb8 ( { Again } 19... b5 $2 { loses to } 20. Naxc5 $1 dxc5 21. Nxc5 Nxc5 22. bxc5 { . } ) 
 ( { The kingside try } 19... f4 20. Bf2 g5 { could be met by the tricky } 21. Qc2 $5 { protecting the knight on a4 , and preparing: } h5 22. bxc5 bxc5 23. Ndxc5 $1 Rxb1 24. Qxb1 dxc5 25. d6 Qd8 26. dxe7 Qxe7 27. Nc3 $16 { With a big advantage due to Black’s weaknesses on d5 , c5 and a6 . } ) 
20. Rb2 Nc8 ( { Once again } 20... b5 $2 { loses to } 21. Naxc5 $1 { as above. } ) 
21. Rc1 Kh7 22. Qc2 Ne7 23. Nf2 Rc8 24. Qb1 Rcb8 25. Bd3 $1 { White wants to force Black to advance ...f5-f4, thus releasing the tension on the kingside, and giving White a free hand on the queenside. } Rf8 { Keeping the tension. } ( { If } 25... f4 26. Bd2 g5 { then White gets a big advantage after } 27. Qc2 h5 28. Nc3 Ng6 29. Rcb1 $16 { since Black’s kingside play is not threatening. White exerts strong pressure on the queenside, though it is not obvious how to exploit the advantage. } ) 
26. Rb3 $1 { The beginning of a simple yet beautiful idea. White will triple his major pieces on the b-file, which will force Black’s rook to return to b8, letting go of the tension on the kingside. } Kg8 27. Rc2 Rfb8 28. Rcb2 { Once again, the f5-pawn is under attack, but now Black has nothing better than pushing ...f5-f4. } f4 29. Bd2 Nc8 30. Rc2 { A good prophylactic move. } ( { Instead } 30. Bf1 $2 { would have allowed } b5 $1 31. Nc3 bxc4 32. Bxc4 cxb4 33. Rxb4 Rxb4 34. Rxb4 Rxb4 35. Qxb4 Bb7 $14 { when Black is back in the game. } ) 
g5 ( { The try } 30... b5 $2 { now fails to } 31. cxb5 axb5 32. Nxc5 $1 dxc5 33. bxc5 { threatening c5-c6, and now } Nxc5 34. Bb4 Bf8 35. Rbc3 { when White emerges a pawn up and with a decisive positional advantage. } ) 
31. Bf1 Qd8 32. g3 $1 { ± Bringing the light-squared bishop into the game via h3, which demolishes all of Black’s hopes to resist with passive defence. White’s position is so solid he does not even need to worry about a kingside attack. } Nf8 { More passive defence will soon lead Black’s position to collapse. } ( { Maybe it was time to attempt } 32... b5 $5 { though after } 33. Nb2 Qc7 34. Nfd3 { Black must continue waiting, since both pawn captures worsen his position: } cxb4 ( { or } 34... bxc4 35. Nxc4 Qd8 36. Bh3 $5 { +- } ) 
35. axb4 bxc4 36. Nxc4 Qd8 37. Na5 Rc7 38. Nc6 $18 ) 
33. Bh3 Qe8 34. Nb2 Rf7 35. Kg2 { Protecting the f3-pawn. } Rc7 36. Nbd3 { Threatening 37.bxc5 as 37...Rxc5 is no longer possible. According to my database this is the last move in the game, though if play had continued White would have realized his advantage very soon. I like the following continuation: } Rbb7 ( { Or: } 36... cxb4 37. axb4 Nd7 38. c5 $1 { The decisive break. } bxc5 39. bxc5 Rxb3 40. Qxb3 { With winning queenside play, say after } dxc5 41. d6 c4 42. Rxc4 Rxc4 43. Qxc4 Kh7 44. Qc7 ) 
37. bxc5 bxc5 ( { Or: } 37... dxc5 38. Bxc8 Qxc8 39. Rxb6 $18 ) 
38. Rcb2 { Gaining the b-file. } Rxb3 39. Rxb3 Nd7 40. Ba5 Ra7 41. Rb8 { +- Invading the 8th rank! } ( { But not } 41. Bxd7 $2 Qxd7 42. Rb8 { due to } Rb7 { when most of the advantage disappears. } ) 
Bf8 ( { The point is that } 41... Nxb8 $2 { loses to } 42. Qxb8 { . } ) 
42. Bxd7 Qxd7 43. Ng4 Bg7 { There should be many ways to impose the advantage, but I like the strong tactical blow: } 44. Ngxe5 Bxe5 ( 44... dxe5 { doesn’t help either: } 45. Nxc5 Qe8 46. Bb6 Re7 47. Rxa8 $18 ) 
45. Nxe5 dxe5 46. Qb6 { Threatening Rxc8. And now the game could have been over after the amusing variation: } Rb7 47. Qg6+ Kf8 48. Qxh6+ Kf7 ( { Or } 48... Kg8 49. Qxg5 Kf8 50. Rxa8 { and wins. } ) 
49. Qh7+ Ke8 50. Rxc8+ Qxc8 51. Qg8+ Kd7 52. Qe6# { Final remarks 1. Black’s 10...a6 weakened the b6-square permanently and this weakening proved important on many occasions throughout the game. 2. White took advantage of the b6-square when playing 13. Qb3, to threaten 14. bxc5 Nxc5 15. Qb6. 3. Later, Black had to double rooks on the b-file precisely because the b6-square was weak, otherwise there would have been no entry points and Black’s rooks could have stayed on the a- and f-files doing their jobs. 4. White’s most remarkable move was 32. g3, bringing the light-squared bishop onto a powerful diagonal. This move however, only worked well because Black’s forces were far from the kingside. 5. White’s position after move 36 displays an ideal arrangement of his forces, and in fact Black’s position is already desperate despite having a closed position and equal material. The rest is a matter of technique, and the sample variation offered is just an illustration of White’s many winning methods. } 
 1-0

