 { Krishnan Sasikiran – Swayangsu Satyapragyan, Calcutta 2009 Learning objective: This game is an example of White’s central break f2-f4, and the subsequent kingside attack. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 e5 4. Nc3 d6 5. e4 Be7 6. g3 O-O 7. Bg2 Nbd7 8. Nge2 Ne8 9. O-O g6 10. Bh6 Ng7 { I was able to find 70 games with this position in my database, where White scored over 70%. This position was first played in 1961, and for a couple of decades it was used by strong players, such as Ivkov or Fedorov. Throughout the last decade, this position has essentially disappeared from master-level games, and today it is only rarely used by players with around a 2200 rating. During this chapter we have learned about the solidity of Black’s position, and the reader might be tempted to think this is always the case, but here we have a clear counterexample. Unlike previous games, Black’s bishop is located on e7 instead of the desirable g7, and White’s pieces are well arranged for the break f2-f4. As it turns out, Black is simply not prepared to meet this break, and White already has a serious advantage if he proceeds correctly. } 11. Qd2 { The most precise; this move and the next prepare the strong f2-f4 break. } ( { Another option was the direct } 11. f4 $5 { but after } exf4 12. gxf4 f5 { the position is rather complex. } ) 
Kh8 { Black is playing schematically; this move clears the g8-square for the knight manoeuvre ...Nf6-g8 chasing away White’s bishop. This is conceptually good, but way too slow. } ( { If } 11... f5 { then White can play in the spirit of the game Wojtaszek – Fedorov with } 12. exf5 gxf5 13. f4 $5 e4 14. g4 $1 { ± when White has promising attacking chances on the kingside. } ) 
12. Rae1 Nf6 13. h3 Ng8 14. Be3 f5 15. f4 $1 { ± } Bf6 { Black wants to recapture on e5 with his bishop in order to preserve a healthy pawn structure, but this is not the best approach, as Black’s problems are far more serious than that. } ( { A healthier option was } 15... Nf6 16. fxe5 dxe5 17. Rf2 { when Black has severe structural problems, but is likely to put up some resistance. For example: } Rb8 ( { but not } 17... b6 $2 18. exf5 gxf5 19. d6 $18 ) 
18. b3 a6 19. Nc1 Bd6 20. Nd3 Qc7 $16 { when White’s position is much to be preferred, but Black is holding for now. } ) 
 ( { Previously } 15... exf4 { had been played, but after: } 16. Bxf4 g5 17. Be3 Bf6 18. exf5 ( 17... Bf6 17. Bf2 $5 { preparing exf5 followed by g3-g4, also puts Black in serious trouble } ) 
Nxf5 { White has the simple } 19. Ne4 $5 Nxe3 20. Qxe3 $16 { when Black’s position could collapse anytime. } ) 
16. fxe5 Bxe5 17. Bf4 $1 { A very important move; Sasikiran clears some lines and trades an important defensive piece. } Bxf4 $6 { After this trade the weak d6-pawn becomes an additional chronic problem which Black cannot cope with. } ( { It was necessary to accept a weak pawn structure and play } 17... Nf6 { though after } 18. Bxe5 dxe5 19. Qe3 Nd7 20. exf5 gxf5 21. Nc1 $16 { White has a huge long-term advantage due to the weakened e5- and f5-pawns, as well as his strong protected passed pawn on d5 . } ) 
18. Nxf4 fxe4 ( 18... Bd7 { helps little, since after } 19. exf5 $1 Nxf5 20. Ne4 { the threats Qc3 or g4 , followed by Nxd6 decide the game: } Nf6 21. g4 Nxe4 22. Bxe4 Nh4 ( { not } 22... Ng7 $2 23. Nxg6 $1 hxg6 24. Qh6 Kg8 25. Bxg6 $18 ) 
23. Ne6 Rxf1 24. Rxf1 Qe7 25. Ng5 { Black’s knight is essentially lost on h4 . } ) 
19. g4 $1 { +- Covering the f5-square, thus preventing Black’s bishop and knight from coming into the game. Black is lost because he lacks mobility and development, and because White will develop multiple tactical threats against the d6-pawn, the e6-square and the king. } ( { Also good enough was: } 19. Nxe4 Bf5 20. Ne6 Nxe6 21. dxe6 Bxe4 22. Qc3 Rf6 23. Bxe4 $18 ) 
e3 { There is nothing better. } ( { The natural-looking } 19... Nf6 { is refuted by } 20. Nxg6 $1 hxg6 21. Qh6 { when Black’s forces are so poorly coordinated they cannot offer resistance despite having an extra piece. For example: } Nh7 22. Nxe4 { (threatening Ng5) } Kg8 23. Qxg6 Rxf1 24. Rxf1 Qe7 25. Nxd6 $18 { Threatening Rf7 or Be4 . } ) 
20. Rxe3 Bd7 ( 20... Nf6 { is also inadequate due to } 21. Ne6 Bxe6 22. dxe6 Qe7 23. Bxb7 $1 { since } Qxb7 { loses to: } 24. e7 Rfe8 25. Rxf6 Rxe7 26. Qxd6 $1 { +- } Rxe3 $2 27. Rf8 Rxf8 28. Qxf8 ) 
21. Ne4 { The concrete threats begin; Black just does not have enough space to organize a defence. } Qc7 ( 21... Qe7 $2 { loses to } 22. Nxc5 { . } ) 
 ( 21... Be8 { loses after: } 22. Ne6 Rxf1 23. Kxf1 Qe7 ( { or } 23... Nxe6 24. dxe6 ) 
24. Nxg7 Qxg7 ( 24... Kxg7 25. Nxc5 Qf7 26. Rf3 ) 
25. Nxd6 ) 
22. Ref3 { Threatening Nxg6. } Rfe8 23. Ng5 Re7 ( { Also hopeless is } 23... Nh6 { due to } 24. Nxg6 $1 hxg6 25. Ne6 $1 Bxe6 26. Qxh6 Kg8 27. dxe6 { . } ) 
24. Nf7+ $1 { A very nice finish. } Rxf7 25. Nxg6+ hxg6 26. Rxf7 { Black resigns since he does not have a satisfactory defence against Qc3xg7#. Final remarks 1. Once the e- and f-pawns are off the board, White will generally have an edge, though it could be small. 2. White’s advantage is due to his spatial advantage, the vulnerable d6-pawn, and the e6-square. In this game White converted these factors into a beautiful win. } 
